Course: INSY 8311 – Database Development with PL/SQL
Instructor: Eric Maniraguha


1. Problems statement
Livestock farming is an important part of agriculture in Rwanda.
It provides milk, meat, eggs, income, and employment for many households. However, 
analyzing livestock production and productivity can be difficult when information about animal populations, 
production levels, and farming activities is stored in different records or reports. Without a well-organized system, 
it can be difficult to compare production across livestock types, districts, and years or to identify changes in productivity.

This project aims to develop a Livestock Production and Productivity Analysis System using Oracle Database and PL/SQL. 
The system will store and organize livestock-related information and generate reports that help users understand production levels and productivity trends. 
It will focus on analyzing available information about cattle, goats, sheep, pigs, and poultry, as well as products such as milk, meat, and eggs.


2.Proposed Solution

The proposed system will provide a central database for storing and managing livestock production information. 
Users will be able to record and view information about livestock types, agricultural households or farms, 
locations, animal populations, and production records. The system will also calculate selected productivity indicators, 
such as average milk production per cow and changes in production over time.

The system will generate reports that compare livestock production by animal type, location, 
and reporting period. It will use PL/SQL procedures, functions, triggers, cursors, and exception handling to manage records, 
apply business rules, and calculate production indicators. An interactive dashboard will present important results in a clear format.

3. Planned Database Tables
The system is expected to include the following tables:
Farm – stores farm or agricultural household identification and location information.
Location – stores province, district, and sector information.
Livestock_Type – stores livestock categories such as cattle, goats, sheep, pigs, and poultry.
Livestock_Population – stores the number of animals by type, farm or area, and reporting period.
Production_Record – stores production information for milk, meat, eggs, and other available livestock products.
Product_Type – stores the different livestock products and their units of measurement.
Reporting_Period – stores the year or period associated with each record.

4. Planned PL/SQL Features
Stored Procedures: To insert, update, and manage livestock and production records.
Functions: To calculate productivity indicators, production totals, and average production.
Triggers: To validate selected records and maintain automatic database updates when required.
Cursors: To process and summarize production records for reports.
Collections and Records: To organize and process groups of livestock and production information.
Exception Handling: To manage invalid entries, missing records, and other database errors.
Bulk Processing: To process multiple production records efficiently.

Expected Results

The project is expected to produce a working Oracle database system that organizes livestock production information and supports productivity analysis. It will provide reports and an interactive dashboard to help users compare production, observe trends, and identify areas that may require further attention.








